# Keeps HOST build output out of the Docker build context (BUG-77).
#
# WHAT GOES WRONG WITHOUT THIS. deploy/Dockerfile restores inside the image, correctly, producing
# /build/src/.../obj/project.assets.json with container paths. It then COPYs the source directories
# wholesale, which sweeps in the host's obj/ and OVERWRITES that file with one naming
# /Users/<someone>/.nuget/packages. The publish step runs --no-restore, so nothing repairs it and the
# build fails inside the container while the same commit builds fine on the host.
#
# WHY IT IS WORSE THAN IT SOUNDS, and why this file is worth its comment: bin/ and obj/ are
# GITIGNORED, so `git status` reports a clean tree while the context is contaminated. Every habit for
# "am I building from a known state" answers yes. The failure then names BouncyCastle and
# SessionKeyExchange, so it reads as a real regression in recently-changed crypto code rather than as
# machine state. The reporter nearly took it that way.
#
# This file lives at the REPOSITORY ROOT because that is the build context for both ways the image is
# produced: `docker build -f deploy/Dockerfile .` and deploy/compose.yaml, which sets `context: ..`.
# Docker reads .dockerignore from the context root, not from beside the Dockerfile, so a copy in
# deploy/ would be silently inert.
#
# DELIBERATELY NARROW. This excludes build output and nothing else. It is not a
# make-the-context-smaller file: every additional pattern is a chance to exclude something the image
# turns out to need, and that failure looks exactly like this bug did. Nothing tracked lives under
# bin/ or obj/ (verified: `git ls-files` matches zero such paths), so these two lines cannot remove
# an input the build requires.
**/bin/
**/obj/
